  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(0)=3,$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=(5x-3)^{2}(4x-2)^{4},$ then ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=(5(0)-3)^{2}(4(0)-2)^{4}=144.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+144x.\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & \u2248L(2) \\\\  & =3+144(2) \\\\  & =3+288 \\\\  & =291.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
